--- a/docs/en/reference/environment.md
+++ b/docs/en/reference/environment.md
@@ -31,3 +31,18 @@ It is *highly* inadvisable to generate production apps while this environment va
 Both macOS and Windows provide mechanisms for running x86_64 binaries on ARM64 hardware. These tools can make it very difficult to generate a packaged application, as it can be difficult to ensure a packaged application contains code matching the right architecture when the platform provides misleading answers about the current platform. As a result, Briefcase will raise an error if it detects that the version of Python that is being used doesn't match the underlying hardware architecture.
 
 However, it is possible to disable this check by setting the `BRIEFCASE_ALLOW_EMULATION` environment variable. This will cause Briefcase to output a warning if it appears that Python is running in emulation mode, but it will allow Briefcase commands to run.
+
+### HTTP and SSL configuration
+
+When Briefcase downloads support files, tools and SDKs, it uses `httpx2`. `httpx2` honors the following environment variables to configure HTTP requests. These can be useful if your computer is behind a proxy, or if it uses a private certificate authority.
+
+- `SSL_CERT_FILE` - The path to a file containing the CA certificates used to verify HTTPS connections.
+- `SSL_CERT_DIR` - The path to a directory containing the CA certificates used to verify HTTPS connections.
+- `HTTP_PROXY` - The URL of the proxy to use for HTTP requests.
+- `HTTPS_PROXY` - The URL of the proxy to use for HTTPS requests.
+- `ALL_PROXY` - The URL of the proxy to use for all requests, regardless of the scheme of the request.
+- `NO_PROXY` - A comma-separated list of hosts that should be accessed without using a proxy.
+
+For full details on the format and behavior of these variables, see [the httpx2 documentation](https://pydantic.dev/docs/httpx2/api/environment_variables/).
+
+If `SSL_CERT_FILE` or `SSL_CERT_DIR` is defined, it must point at an existing file or directory. If it doesn't, Briefcase will raise an error when it attempts to download anything.
